<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Cosiata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cosiata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Cosiata was a political movement and revolt in Venezuela in 1826, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> against the government of Gran Colombia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>. It began when Congress summoned Páez to answer charges, and Venezuelan towns backed his refusal. Bolívar returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and pardoned Páez in 1827, but the revolt foreshadowed Venezuela's secession in 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-cosiata.jpg" alt="La Cosiata" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Antonio_P%C3%A1ez_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-cucuta">Constitution of Cúcuta</a> of 1821 was resented in Venezuela from the beginning and was enacted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> only under protest from the municipality. Venezuelans also objected to the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> as vice-president, the choice of a centralist rather than a federal system and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> as capital, and the constitution could not be reformed for ten years. On 31 August 1824 Santander decreed a general enlistment of all citizens aged 16 to 50 and demanded 50,000 men from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Venezuela, fearing royalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> and a joint army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a>, General Commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Venezuela since 1822, delayed the decree for almost a year. When he finally called up recruits, only 800 came, and further calls made with Brigadier General Juan Escalona ended in bloody disturbances. Reports of excesses reached the Municipal Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, whose accusation was taken up in the Senate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, which removed Páez from his post and summoned him to stand trial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Páez handed command to Escalona but, warned by Miguel Peña among others that going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> would lead him to the scaffold, refused to obey and stayed at his house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. Peña cited the case of Colonel Leonardo Infante, a Venezuelan officer sentenced to be shot in March 1825. The movement, also called the Revolution of the Morrocoyes, broke out in Valencia on 30 April 1826: the mayors declared that Páez must be brought back to lead the country, and he declared himself in rebellion. According to the historian José M. Ameliach, the name came from an Italian comedian's word for trivial theatrical events.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bolívar left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 4 September 1826 and, by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a> on 31 December. By 19 January 1827 he had decreed that the acts of those who took part be forgotten; he met Páez, granted him an amnesty and confirmed him as civil and military chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Venezuela. Divisions between Venezuelan and New Granadan leaders grew as reform was delayed. After the Ocaña Convention of 1828 and Bolívar's centralist constitution of August that year, an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> rejected his authority on 27 December 1829, and a constituent congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> from 6 May 1830 created the Republic of Venezuela.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Cosiata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1826 Congress summoned Páez to answer charges of abuse of authority; he refused and launched the rebellion known as <b>La Cosiata</b> in Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Venezuelan elites resented government from Bogotá, and in 1826 José Antonio Páez, the llanero general who commanded in Venezuela, defied the central government in the revolt known as <b>La Cosiata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
